  "claim": "Johnny Mathis Sings included a song that started out as the instrumental title track to a film directed by Blake Edwards.",
  "evidencia": "Page: Johnny Mathis Sings. Sentence: The finished product included a number from Broadway's \"The Roar of the Greasepaint—the Smell of the Crowd\", a cover of the Beatles hit \"Eleanor Rigby\", two offerings from songwriters Burt Bacharach and Hal David, and three cuts that originated in film scores but had lyrics added later: the melody for \"Strangers in the Night\" was written for \"A Man Could Get Killed\"; \"Somewhere My Love\" began as \"Lara's Theme\" from \"Doctor Zhivago\"; and \"Lovers in New York\" started out as the instrumental title track from \"Breakfast at Tiffany's\".\nPage: Breakfast at Tiffany's (film). Sentence: Breakfast at Tiffany's is a 1961 American romantic comedy film directed by Blake Edwards and written by George Axelrod, loosely based on Truman Capote's novella of the same name.",
  "evidence_text": "Page: Johnny Mathis Sings. Sentence: The finished product included a number from Broadway's \"The Roar of the Greasepaint—the Smell of the Crowd\", a cover of the Beatles hit \"Eleanor Rigby\", two offerings from songwriters Burt Bacharach and Hal David, and three cuts that originated in film scores but had lyrics added later: the melody for \"Strangers in the Night\" was written for \"A Man Could Get Killed\"; \"Somewhere My Love\" began as \"Lara's Theme\" from \"Doctor Zhivago\"; and \"Lovers in New York\" started out as the instrumental title track from \"Breakfast at Tiffany's\".\nPage: Breakfast at Tiffany's (film). Sentence: Breakfast at Tiffany's is a 1961 American romantic comedy film directed by Blake Edwards and written by George Axelrod, loosely based on Truman Capote's novella of the same name.",
