        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "b449e699-a90a-5f06-8c4c-d0eb5e007122",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Johnny Mathis Sings, Breakfast at Tiffany's\n- People: Johnny Mathis, Blake Edwards, George Axelrod, Truman Capote, Burt Bacharach, Hal David, The Beatles\n- Topics: Broadway songs, film scores, cover songs\n\nFacts:\n- Breakfast at Tiffany's is a 1961 American romantic comedy film directed by Blake Edwards and written by George Axelrod, and it was loosely based on Truman Capote's novella of the same name.\n- The finished product of Johnny Mathis Sings included a number from the Broadway musical The Roar of the Greasepaint—the Smell of the Crowd.\n- Johnny Mathis Sings included a cover of the Beatles hit Eleanor Rigby.\n- Johnny Mathis Sings included two songs by songwriters Burt Bacharach and Hal David.\n- Johnny Mathis Sings included Lovers in New York, which originated as the instrumental title track from the film Breakfast at Tiffany's.\n- Johnny Mathis Sings included Strangers in the Night, whose melody was originally written for A Man Could Get Killed.\n- Johnny Mathis Sings included Somewhere My Love, which began as Lara's Theme from Doctor Zhivago.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "2c84ce04-455f-5c42-949b-aba886b894af",
        "text": "Page: Johnny Mathis Sings. Sentence: The finished product included a number from Broadway's \"The Roar of the Greasepaint—the Smell of the Crowd\", a cover of the Beatles hit \"Eleanor Rigby\", two offerings from songwriters Burt Bacharach and Hal David, and three cuts that originated in film scores but had lyrics added later: the melody for \"Strangers in the Night\" was written for \"A Man Could Get Killed\"; \"Somewhere My Love\" began as \"Lara's Theme\" from \"Doctor Zhivago\"; and \"Lovers in New York\" started out as the instrumental title track from \"Breakfast at Tiffany's\".\nPage: Breakfast at Tiffany's (film). Sentence: Breakfast at Tiffany's is a 1961 American romantic comedy film directed by Blake Edwards and written by George Axelrod, loosely based on Truman Capote's novella of the same name.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
